Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Cervical motion tenderness, Bilateral adnexal/uterine tenderness, Purulent vaginal discharge

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-029
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower pelvic pain, deep pain with intercourse, and new-onset right upper quadrant pain.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, bilateral pelvic pain, fever, and new-onset sharp right upper quadrant pain in a young female. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to diagnose Pelvic Inflammatory Disease, rule out acute cholecystitis, and specifically assess for Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (perihepatitis) to initiate immediate, aggressive antibiotic therapy before the next available in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My lower stomach has been aching since my period ended, but tonight the pain is severe, and now I have a sharp pain under my right ribs when I breathe deep.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began gradually three days ago, originating shortly after the cessation of her menses. The patient reports worsening bilateral lower abdominal and pelvic pain. Tonight, she developed a new, sharp right upper quadrant pain that catches when she takes a deep breath. She reports a high fever (38.8 C at home) and chills. She noted an abnormal, purulent, and malodorous vaginal discharge starting yesterday. She reports recent severe dyspareunia (specifically deep pain with intercourse) and abnormal postcoital spotting over the weekend. She also notes increased urinary frequency.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but moving very cautiously; splinting her right side when breathing deeply.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; risk of progressive systemic infection.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 38.9 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient appears visibly unwell, flushed, tachypneic (due to pleuritic pain), and holding her right side.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals bilateral lower quadrant tenderness with voluntary guarding. There is distinct, sharp tenderness in the right upper quadrant, specifically over the liver capsule, exacerbated by deep inspiration (positive pleuritic catch), highly indicative of perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome). No Murphy's sign (no gallbladder arrest). Speculum examination shows profuse, purulent cervical discharge. Bimanual pelvic exam reveals pronounced cervical motion tenderness (chandelier sign) and severe bilateral adnexal and uterine tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took ibuprofen 400mg with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) with associated Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (Perihepatitis) - Status: Severe, systemic involvement
Differentials:

Acute Cholecystitis / Biliary Colic - Ruled out as the primary cause due to the overwhelming presence of classic pelvic signs (cervical motion tenderness, purulent discharge, bilateral adnexal pain) and the pleuritic nature of the RUQ pain (capsular inflammation).

Acute Appendicitis - Unlikely given the bilateral pelvic pain, the specific right upper quadrant capsular pain, and the presence of purulent cervical discharge.

Ectopic Pregnancy - Definitively ruled out by a negative point-of-care urine pregnancy test.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) complicated by right upper quadrant pain, a classic presentation of Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (perihepatitis caused by ascending infected fluid irritating the liver capsule). The objective clinical findings of pronounced cervical motion tenderness, purulent discharge, high fever, and specific liver capsule tenderness confirm a severe, disseminated local infection. Urgent after-hours intervention was clinically mandated to immediately administer high-dose parenteral antibiotics (ceftriaxone) to halt the ascending infection. Failing to treat this aggressively tonight would have risked severe, permanent tubal scarring, chronic pelvic pain, and potential progression to a surgical emergency (tubo-ovarian abscess).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular Ceftriaxone 500mg immediately. Prescribed a 14-day course of oral Doxycycline 100mg twice daily and oral Metronidazole 400mg twice daily. Collected endocervical swabs for nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) to identify the specific pathogen (likely Gonorrhea or Chlamydia). Administered oral paracetamol 1g for fever control.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the sharp pain under your ribs, take slow, shallow breaths and hold a pillow against your right side if you need to cough; feeling the physical support will reduce the friction on the inflamed liver capsule. To guarantee the infection is cured, take the antibiotics at the exact same times every day for the full 14 days; establishing this strict routine is the only way to ensure the bacteria are completely eradicated from the deep tissues.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or sexual health clinic review in 48-72 hours to assess clinical response and review swab results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if you become unable to keep the oral antibiotics down due to vomiting, if your fever climbs above 39.5 C, or if the abdominal pain becomes completely rigid and unbearable.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome sounds frightening, but it is a known complication of PID that typically responds very well to this specific antibiotic regimen.
Clinical Course: The sharp pain under your ribs should start to fade within 48 to 72 hours as the antibiotics reduce the inflammation around your liver. The pelvic pain will also gradually improve over the next week.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you start vomiting continuously, or if your abdomen becomes hard, rigid, and intensely painful.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The severe bacterial infection in your pelvis has tracked upwards and caused inflammation on the outside capsule of your liver, which is causing that sharp pain when you breathe deeply. The antibiotic injection tonight is designed to stop this aggressively. You must complete the 14 days of oral tablets to fully clear the infection and prevent permanent damage to your fallopian tubes.
Actionable Steps: Start the oral antibiotics tonight. Strictly avoid all alcohol while on metronidazole. Use a pillow to splint your right side when moving, and ensure you attend your 48-hour follow-up appointment.

